# How we meet 👥

At Ultralytics we move fast, act with urgency, and rely on collaboration to do our best work. Meetings are a regular part of how we push forward, and how we manage them matters. <br/>
Industry-wide, workers spend the equivalent of 10 full work weeks every year in meetings, and [60%](https://www.flowtrace.co/collaboration-blog/50-meeting-statistics) of those lack a structured agenda. We believe there’s a better way to meet.

![Visual diagram of four-stage meeting best practice.](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1UFp5DlgoaZsOTppmSksHT_UikWJ4G3A4/view?usp=sharing)

## Prepare

> *Proper planning ensures that meetings are actually needed and the right people are ready.*

+ **Set the objective:** Clearly state why the meeting is happening. If it is purely for status updates that could be sent via email, cancel the meeting.
+ **Share the agenda:** List specific topics to cover. Assign time limits to each agenda item to keep the discussion on track. Distribute the agenda at least 24–48 hours in advance so participants can prepare.
+ **Schedule mindfully:** When scheduling across multiple regions, use tools like World Time Buddy to find an overlap that respects everyone’s local working hours, rotating inconvenient times if necessary for recurring meetings.
+ **Minimize:** Keep the group small. Only include people who need to contribute directly or make decisions. Set the meeting only as long as is needed.

## Setup

> *A smooth setup eliminates wasted time at the start of the call.*

+ **Arrive Punctually:** Test your tech a few minutes early so the meeting starts on time.
+ **Room & Equipment Logistics:** For hybrid meetings, test the conference room microphone, camera, and display screen ahead of time.
  + Ensure all links (video call, shared docs) are included in the calendar invitation.
+ **Video Call Hygiene:** For virtual meetings we turn our cameras on.
  + Lighting & Framing: Position your camera at eye level and ensure your face is well-lit (avoid sitting directly in front of a bright window).
  + Virtual Backgrounds: Use a clean, neutral background or blur it if your physical space is cluttered or distracting.
  + Audio Discipline: Mute your microphone when you are not speaking to avoid background noise. Use a headset if you are in a busy environment.
+ **Use a Notetaker:** There are some meetings where this might not be appropriate, but for most internal meetings we use AI notetakers as standard.

## Participate

  > *Active and respectful participation maximizes the value of the meeting time.*

+ **Engage Actively:** Contribute your perspective, ask clarifying questions, and build constructively on the ideas of others.
+ **Practice Inclusive Communication:** If you notice quieter team members haven't spoken, invite their input.
  + :raised_hand: Avoid interrupting others; use the "raise hand" feature in virtual platforms for larger groups.
+ **Stay Focused:** Close out unrelated browser tabs, silence notifications, and avoid the [myth of multitasking](https://www.neuroleadership.com/articles/the-myth-of-multitasking).

## Follow up

> *The meeting doesn't end when the call disconnects; execution happens afterward.*

+ **Distribute Meeting Notes Promptly:** Send out a summary within 24 hours while details are fresh.
+ **Document Action Items (The "Who, What, and When"):**
  + Every decision and assigned task must have a clear owner.
  + Attach a specific deadline to each action item.
+ **Archive Materials:** Store the recording, transcript, presentation slides, and shared notes in a centralized, easily accessible folder for team reference.

<br/>

By aligning on these simple best practices we show up prepared, respect one another’s time, and reduce passive meeting attendance. That leaves more time to build, experiment and grow the business.
